-- title:
--   The modular ring as the range of two-variable evaluation
-- statement:
--   Fix a natural number $N$ with $N \neq 0$, a commutative ring $L$, and a subring $A \subseteq L$. Two subrings of the Laurent series ring $L((q))$ are compared. The first, `CharPReduction.modularRing N A`, is the subring generated by the union of the image of the ring homomorphism `constSeries A : A →+* LaurentSeries L` — the inclusion $A \hookrightarrow L$ followed by the structure map sending an element of $L$ to the corresponding constant Laurent series — with the two-element set consisting of `jqModC L`, namely $q^{-1}$ times the image in $L((q))$ of the integral power series `jNum` under the map induced by $\mathbb{Z} \to L$, and `jqNModC L N`, the series obtained from `jqModC L` by the operation `qExpand L N`. The second is the range of the ring homomorphism `NodeLocalized.modularEval N A` on $A$-polynomials in two variables, i.e. the evaluation homomorphism $\mathrm{MvPolynomial}\,(\mathrm{Fin}\,2)\,A \to L((q))$ determined by `constSeries A` on coefficients and by sending the variable indexed $0$ to `jqModC L` and the variable indexed $1$ to `jqNModC L N`. The theorem asserts that these two subrings are equal.
--
--   This records the interchangeability of the two presentations of the modular ring $A[j(q), j(q^N)] \subseteq L((q))$: as a generated subring, and as the set of values $P(j(q), j(q^N))$ of two-variable polynomials with coefficients in $A$. It is used where an element of the modular ring must be named by an explicit polynomial with finitely many coefficients, for instance in the results on localisation at a point of the modular curve and on prolongations in the place-specialisation arguments.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_modularRing_eq_range_modularEval.lean

